<% content_for :title, "Buddhism | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="spirituality-buddhism-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="spirituality-buddhism-hero">
    <div class="spirituality-buddhism-hero-content">

      <p class="spirituality-buddhism-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / SPIRITUALITY / BUDDHISM
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>YOU DON'T</span>
        <span>HAVE TO</span>
        <span>ESCAPE THIS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="spirituality-buddhism-intro">
        SUFFERING. CHANGE. COMPASSION.<br>
        LEARNING HOW TO BE HERE.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="spirituality-buddhism-content">

    <!-- 01 / INTRODUCTION -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-black">

      <p class="sb-number">01 / THE BASIC IDEA</p>

      <h2>
        LIFE IS<br>
        FUCKING HARD.
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          You experience pain, loss, disappointment, uncertainty,
          and change. Even your happiest experiences shift.
          You cannot make everything you love stay exactly
          as it is.
        </p>

        <p>
          Buddhism invites you to examine your relationship
          with that reality. Where do you cling to pleasure?
          Where do you fight what has already happened?
          What happens when life refuses to follow your expectations?
        </p>

        <p>
          Through wisdom, ethical conduct, and mental cultivation,
          you practice understanding suffering and loosening
          the patterns that keep it going.
        </p>

        <div class="sb-callout">
          WHAT ARE YOU EXPERIENCING?<br>
          WHAT CONTRIBUTES TO IT?<br>
          WHAT HELPS?
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 02 / FOUR NOBLE TRUTHS -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-pink">

      <p class="sb-number">02 / THE FOUR NOBLE TRUTHS</p>

      <h2>
        START WITH<br>
        WHAT HURTS.
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          The Four Noble Truths give you a framework for
          investigating suffering: recognize it, understand
          its origin, recognize the possibility of its cessation,
          and cultivate the path toward that freedom.
        </p>

        <div class="sb-grid">

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>01 / DUKKHA</span>
            <h3>RECOGNIZE YOUR SUFFERING.</h3>
            <p>
              Acknowledge pain and dissatisfaction.
              Notice how changing experiences cannot give
              you permanent security.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>02 / ORIGIN</span>
            <h3>UNDERSTAND YOUR CRAVING.</h3>
            <p>
              Examine your thirst for pleasure, becoming
              something, or getting rid of an experience.
              Notice how clinging sustains suffering.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>03 / CESSATION</span>
            <h3>RECOGNIZE THE POSSIBILITY.</h3>
            <p>
              You can cultivate freedom through
              relinquishing craving and attachment.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>04 / PATH</span>
            <h3>PRACTICE THE WAY.</h3>
            <p>
              Develop wisdom, ethical conduct, and mental
              discipline through the Noble Eightfold Path.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 03 / EIGHTFOLD PATH -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-yellow">

      <p class="sb-number">03 / THE NOBLE EIGHTFOLD PATH</p>

      <h2>
        PUT IT<br>
        INTO<br>
        PRACTICE.
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          You cultivate these eight practices together.
          Your understanding influences your conduct,
          and your conduct affects the mind you bring
          to practice.
        </p>

        <div class="sb-grid">

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>01 / WISDOM</span>
            <h3>RIGHT VIEW</h3>
            <p>
              Develop your understanding of suffering,
              its causes, and the consequences of
              your intentional actions.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>02 / WISDOM</span>
            <h3>RIGHT INTENTION</h3>
            <p>
              Orient your intentions toward renunciation,
              goodwill, and non-harming.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>03 / ETHICAL CONDUCT</span>
            <h3>RIGHT SPEECH</h3>
            <p>
              Speak truthfully and with care.
              Refrain from lies, divisive speech,
              cruelty, and idle chatter.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>04 / ETHICAL CONDUCT</span>
            <h3>RIGHT ACTION</h3>
            <p>
              Respect life and others' belongings.
              Practice sexual responsibility and
              consider the harm your actions could cause.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>05 / ETHICAL CONDUCT</span>
            <h3>RIGHT LIVELIHOOD</h3>
            <p>
              Earn your living in ways that avoid
              harming or exploiting other beings.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>06 / MENTAL CULTIVATION</span>
            <h3>RIGHT EFFORT</h3>
            <p>
              Work to prevent and abandon unwholesome
              states while developing and sustaining
              wholesome ones.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>07 / MENTAL CULTIVATION</span>
            <h3>RIGHT MINDFULNESS</h3>
            <p>
              Cultivate attentive awareness of your body,
              feelings, mind, and mental phenomena.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>08 / MENTAL CULTIVATION</span>
            <h3>RIGHT CONCENTRATION</h3>
            <p>
              Develop collected, stable attention through
              meditative practice to support deeper insight.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div class="sb-callout">
          HOW YOU UNDERSTAND.<br>
          HOW YOU ACT.<br>
          HOW YOU PRACTICE.
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 04 / THREE CHARACTERISTICS -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-purple">

      <p class="sb-number">04 / THE THREE CHARACTERISTICS</p>

      <h2>
        NOTHING<br>
        STAYS<br>
        FIXED.
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          You can examine your experience through
          impermanence, unsatisfactoriness, and non-self.
          These teachings invite you to look closely
          at what you ordinarily take for granted.
        </p>

        <div class="sb-grid">

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>01 / ANICCA</span>
            <h3>IMPERMANENCE</h3>
            <p>
              Your thoughts, feelings, body, relationships,
              and circumstances change through causes
              and conditions.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>02 / DUKKHA</span>
            <h3>UNSATISFACTORINESS</h3>
            <p>
              You cannot find permanent satisfaction
              in what changes. Clinging to it leaves
              you vulnerable to distress.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>03 / ANATTA</span>
            <h3>NON-SELF</h3>
            <p>
              Examine whether your changing body and mind
              contain a fixed, independent, unchanging self.
              Notice how much depends on conditions.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <p>
          You may begin to loosen your identification with
          a feeling, role, or story about yourself.
          An experience can matter deeply without becoming
          a permanent definition of who you are.
        </p>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 05 / MINDFULNESS -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-black">

      <p class="sb-number">05 / MINDFULNESS</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT IS<br>
        HAPPENING<br>
        RIGHT NOW?
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          You practice recognizing your experience as it unfolds.
          You attend to sensations, feelings, thoughts,
          and mental patterns with enough clarity to
          investigate what is happening.
        </p>

        <div class="sb-grid">

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>01 / BODY</span>
            <h3>NOTICE YOUR BODY.</h3>
            <p>
              Attend to your breathing, posture,
              movement, and changing physical experience.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>02 / FEELING</span>
            <h3>NOTICE THE TONE.</h3>
            <p>
              Recognize whether your experience feels
              pleasant, unpleasant, or neutral.
              Notice your response.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>03 / MIND</span>
            <h3>NOTICE YOUR STATE.</h3>
            <p>
              Recognize whether your mind is distracted,
              concentrated, angry, attached, or otherwise.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>04 / MENTAL PHENOMENA</span>
            <h3>NOTICE THE PATTERNS.</h3>
            <p>
              Examine your experience through Buddhist
              teachings, including hindrances and
              qualities that support awakening.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <p>
          You can begin by noticing a thought before
          accepting it as fact, or recognizing an emotion
          before acting on it.
        </p>

        <div class="sb-thought">
          YOU NOTICE ANGER.<br>
          WHAT IS FEEDING IT?<br>
          WHAT CHANGES AS YOU PAY ATTENTION?
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 06 / CRAVING AND ATTACHMENT -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-pink">

      <p class="sb-number">06 / CRAVING, AVERSION &amp; ATTACHMENT</p>

      <h2>
        YOU WANT IT.<br>
        KEEP IT.<br>
        MAKE IT STOP.
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          You reach toward pleasure, push away discomfort,
          and hold tightly to whatever seems capable
          of making you secure.
        </p>

        <p>
          Notice how these movements shape your relationship
          with people, possessions, beliefs, and your own identity.
          Listen for the demands underneath your reactions.
        </p>

        <div class="sb-stack">
          <span>YOU NEED THIS FEELING TO STAY.</span>
          <span>YOU NEED THIS DISCOMFORT TO END.</span>
          <span>YOU NEED THIS PERSON TO REMAIN THE SAME.</span>
          <span>YOU NEED YOUR LIFE TO FOLLOW THE PLAN.</span>
        </div>

        <p>
          Practice loosening those demands. You can love,
          enjoy, and commit while allowing more room
          for change and uncertainty.
        </p>

        <div class="sb-callout">
          CARE DEEPLY.<br>
          NOTICE WHERE YOU ARE CLINGING.
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 07 / COMPASSION -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-yellow">

      <p class="sb-number">07 / COMPASSION &amp; LOVING-KINDNESS</p>

      <h2>
        LESS HATRED.<br>
        MORE<br>
        HUMANITY.
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          Through compassion, or karuna, you cultivate
          the wish to relieve suffering. Through
          loving-kindness, or metta, you cultivate
          goodwill toward yourself and other beings.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can recognize suffering while maintaining
          boundaries and taking responsibility.
          Care can help you face harm honestly
          and consider what repair requires.
        </p>

        <div class="sb-grid">

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>KARUNA</span>
            <h3>MEET SUFFERING.</h3>
            <p>
              Acknowledge your own and others' pain.
              Consider what could help alleviate it.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>METTA</span>
            <h3>CULTIVATE GOODWILL.</h3>
            <p>
              Extend wishes for safety, happiness,
              and well-being to yourself and others.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div class="sb-callout">
          OFFER YOURSELF KINDNESS.<br>
          EXTEND IT TO SOMEONE ELSE.<br>
          KEEP PRACTICING.
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 08 / MIDDLE WAY -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-purple">

      <p class="sb-number">08 / THE MIDDLE WAY &amp; EQUANIMITY</p>

      <h2>
        STEP OUT<br>
        OF THE<br>
        EXTREMES.
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          Through the Middle Way, you cultivate a path
          that avoids sensual indulgence and severe
          self-mortification.
        </p>

        <p>
          Examine what your choices produce. Does chasing
          pleasure leave you more dependent on it?
          Does harsh self-denial undermine your health
          and ability to practice?
        </p>

        <p>
          Through equanimity, you develop steadiness amid
          pleasure and pain, praise and criticism,
          gain and loss. You remain engaged while learning
          to be less overwhelmed by each change.
        </p>

        <div class="sb-callout">
          WHAT SUPPORTS YOUR CLARITY?<br>
          WHAT ADDS TO YOUR SUFFERING?
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 09 / CAUSES AND CONDITIONS -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-black">

      <p class="sb-number">09 / DEPENDENT ORIGINATION</p>

      <h2>
        LOOK AT<br>
        WHAT KEEPS<br>
        IT GOING.
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          Dependent origination invites you to understand
          how suffering arises through conditions.
          You examine relationships among ignorance,
          craving, clinging, and the continuation
          of suffering.
        </p>

        <p>
          As you investigate a pattern, you may recognize
          places where changing a condition or your response
          changes what follows.
        </p>

        <div class="sb-stack">
          <span>WHAT CONDITIONS ARE PRESENT?</span>
          <span>WHAT KEEPS YOUR PATTERN GOING?</span>
          <span>WHERE CAN YOU RESPOND DIFFERENTLY?</span>
        </div>

        <p>
          In your everyday life, bring that curiosity
          to your habits, surroundings, relationships,
          and the support available to you.
        </p>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 10 / COURAGE AND ADVERSITY -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-pink">

      <p class="sb-number">10 / WORKING WITH DIFFICULTY</p>

      <h2>
        MEET LIFE<br>
        WITH AN<br>
        OPEN HEART.
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          Tibetan Buddhist mind-training practices offer
          ways for you to work with adversity.
          You can use difficult experiences to examine
          attachment, cultivate patience, and deepen compassion.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can seek relief, protection, and change
          while exploring your reactions.
          Learning from hardship does not require
          you to welcome more of it.
        </p>

        <div class="sb-grid">

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>MIND TRAINING</span>
            <h3>PRACTICE IN DIFFICULTY.</h3>
            <p>
              Notice the habits hardship brings forward.
              Cultivate your capacity to respond with
              patience and compassion.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="sb-mini-card">
            <span>SHAMBHALA</span>
            <h3>GENTLENESS &amp; BRAVERY.</h3>
            <p>
              Explore basic goodness: your capacity
              for wakefulness, dignity, and openness.
              Practice meeting experience with courage.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <p>
          In Shambhala's language of warriorship,
          you develop bravery through openness.
          You can acknowledge fear, remain tender,
          and act with care amid uncertainty.
        </p>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 11 / EVERYDAY PRACTICE -->
    <article class="sb-section sb-section-yellow">

      <p class="sb-number">11 / EVERYDAY PRACTICE</p>

      <h2>
        BRING IT<br>
        INTO YOUR<br>
        ACTUAL LIFE.
      </h2>

      <div class="sb-copy">

        <p>
          Give your practice a place in how you speak,
          work, pay attention, and care for others.
          Begin with actions you can repeat.
        </p>

        <div class="sb-practice-grid">

          <div>
            <span>01</span>
            <p>
              Take a few comfortable breaths.
              Feel your contact with the chair or floor.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>02</span>
            <p>
              Recognize a pleasant, unpleasant, or neutral
              experience before reacting.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>03</span>
            <p>
              Notice where your appreciation has become
              a demand that something stay the same.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>04</span>
            <p>
              Practice a moment of metta toward yourself
              and another person.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>05</span>
            <p>
              Choose words that are truthful,
              useful, and considerate.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>06</span>
            <p>
              Give your attention to something nourishing:
              companionship, creativity, nature, or rest.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <p>
          Practice at a manageable pace. Give yourself
          opportunities to learn through repetition
          and notice how your understanding develops.
        </p>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- ENDING -->
    <article class="sb-ending">

      <p>
        YOUR AWARENESS.<br>
        YOUR PRACTICE.<br>
        YOUR OPEN HEART.
      </p>

      <h2>
        BE HERE.<br>
        NOTICE.<br>
        PRACTICE.
      </h2>

      <p class="sb-ending-copy">
        Learn what feeds your suffering, cultivate
        what supports well-being, and bring that
        understanding into the life in front of you.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO SPIRITUALITY",
          spirituality_path,
          class: "sb-back-link" %>

    </article>

  </section>

</main>